.10197 ... An orthomorphism of a finite group G is a bijection $\\phi \\colon G\\to G$ such that $g\\mapsto g^{-1}\\phi (g)$ is also a bijection. ... Alp Müyesser\n\n# Cycle type in Hall–Paige: a proof of the Friedlander–Gordon–Tannenbaum conjecture\n\nForum of Mathematics, Sigma\n\n  * April 2026\n  * 14\n\nDOI:10.1017/fms.2026.10197\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nAlp Muyesser\n\n  * University College London\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nAn orthomorphism of a finite group G is a bijection $\\phi \\colon G\\to G$ such that $g\\mapsto g^{-1}\\phi (g)$ is also a bijection. In 1981, Friedlander, Gordon, and Tannenbaum conjectured that when G is abelian, for any $k\\geq 2$ dividing $|G|-1$ , there exists an orthomorphism of G fixing the identity and permuting the remaining elements as products of disjoint k -cycles as long as the Sylow 2 -subgroups of G are trivial or noncyclic. We prove this conjecture for all sufficiently large groups.\n\n【8†On the left: A 2-absorber for the 3 vertices contained in boxes (from the proof of Lemma 5.10). \nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nForum of Mathematics, Sigma (2026), Vol. 14:e50 1–36\n\ndoi:10.1017/fms.2026.10197\n\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\n\nCycle type in Hall–Paige: a proof of the Friedlander–Gordon–\n\nTannenbaum conjecture\n\nAlp Müyesser\n\nUniversity College London, UK; E-mail: alp.muyesser@new.ox.ac.uk.\n\nReceived: 28 July 2023; Revised: 18 December 2025; Accepted: 18 December 2025\n\n2020 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation: Primary – 05B15, 20P05\n\nAbstract\n\nAn orthomorphism of a ﬁnite group G is a bijection 𝜙:𝐺→𝐺 such that 𝑔↦→ 𝑔−1 𝜙(𝑔)is also a bijection. In 1981,\n\nFriedlander, Gordon, and Tannenbaum conjectured that when G is abelian, for any 𝑘≥2 dividing |𝐺|−1, there\n\nexists an orthomorphism of G ﬁxing the identity and permuting the remaining elements as products of disjoint\n\nk-cycles as long as the Sylow 2-subgroups of G are trivial or noncyclic. We prove this conjecture for all suﬃciently\n\nlarge groups.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nAn orthomorphism of a ﬁnite group G is a bijection 𝜙:𝐺→𝐺 such that 𝑔↦→ 𝑔−1 𝜙(𝑔)is also\n\nbijective. Orthomorphisms have attracted much interest in recent years, not least due to their link\n\nwith Latin squares. The multiplication tables of groups with orthomorphisms yield Latin squares with\n\northogonal mates, which in turn give useful constructions in design theory (see the book of Evans [15]\n\nfor an overview of the area). A fundamental conjecture in the area is the Hall–Paige conjecture [22]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEpic: property testing, hereditary properties, and local repair for finite relational models · Issue #8 · cameronfreer/regularity-lemmata · GitHub (https://github.com/cameronfreer/regularity-lemmata/issues/8)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; opened on Jul 17, 2026 ... Their principal results: one-sided testability of hereditary directed, polychromatic hypergraph properties; restricted positive local-repairability results; and negative local-repairability results for directed graphs and general undirected 3-uniform hypergraphs.\n\n# Epic: property testing, hereditary properties, and local repair for finite relational models #8\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\nEpic: property testing, hereditary properties, and local repair for finite relational models#8\n\nCopy link\n\nLabels\n\nenhancementNew feature or requestNew feature or request\n\n## Description\n\ncameronfreer\n\nopened on Jul 17, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\nGoal. Extend the finite regularity/removal library toward Austin–Tao-adjacent results — property testing, hereditary properties, and local repair for finite relational models — without claiming to formalize their infinitary proof.\n\nContext. Austin & Tao, Testability and repair of hereditary hypergraph properties (arXiv:0801.2179; author summary). Their principal results: one-sided testability of hereditary directed, polychromatic hypergraph properties; restricted positive local-repairability results; and negative local-repairability results for directed graphs and general undirected 3-uniform hypergraphs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDömötör Pálvölgyi - researchr alias (https://researchr.org/alias/d%C3%B6m%C3%B6t%C3%B6r-p%C3%A1lv%C3%B6lgyi)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * Note on Polychromatic Coloring of Hereditary Hypergraph Families Dömötör Pálvölgyi. gc, 40(6):131, December 2024. [doi]\n## 2024\n\n  * On tangencies among planar curves with an application to coloring L-shapesEyal Ackerman, Balázs Keszegh, Dömötör Pálvölgyi. ejc, 121:103837, 2024. [doi]\n\n  * The double Hall property and cycle covers in bipartite graphsJános Barát, Andrzej Grzesik, Attila Jung, Zoltán Lóránt Nagy, Dömötör Pálvölgyi. DM, 347(9):114079, 2024. [doi]\n\n  * Odd-sunflowersPeter Frankl, János Pach, Dömötör Pálvölgyi. jcta, 206:105889, 2024. [doi]\n\n  * Note on Polychromatic Coloring of Hereditary Hypergraph FamiliesDömötör Pálvölgyi. gc, 40(6):131, December 2024. [doi]\n\n  * Orientation of Convex SetsPéter Ágoston, Gábor Damásdi, Balázs Keszegh, Dömötör Pálvölgyi. combinatorics, 31(4), 2024. [doi]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-11-19&page=53&range=182)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Sep 29 2026 cs.CC arXiv:2609.34029v2 ... @misc{2609.33498, author = {Robin Reuvers}, title = {{M}inimal output entropy for the channels that add or remove a box of a {Y}oung diagram, and a {P}auli principle for every permutation symmetry}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.33498}, note = {arXiv:2609.33498v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Two irreducible representations of $U(d)$ whose Young diagrams differ by a single box are connected by four covariant quantum channels: the box can be removed or added, and one keeps either the new diagram or the box ($\\mathbb{C}^d$ or its dual).\n\nRobin Reuvers\n\nSep 29 2026 math-ph math.MP quant-ph arXiv:2609.33498v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n3\n\n@misc{2609.33498, author = {Robin Reuvers}, title = {{M}inimal output entropy for the channels that add or remove a box of a {Y}oung diagram, and a {P}auli principle for every permutation symmetry}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.33498}, note = {arXiv:2609.33498v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nTwo irreducible representations of $U(d)$ whose Young diagrams differ by a single box are connected by four covariant quantum channels: the box can be removed or added, and one keeps either the new diagram or the box ($\\mathbb{C}^d$ or its dual). We prove that for each of these channels the output of a coherent state majorizes every other output, so that coherent states minimize the output entropy, and we compute the optimal output explicitly in terms of hook lengths. For the two channels that keep the box we also determine all minimizers; these need not be coherent, even when they are pure. As an application we consider $N$ particles with a given permutation symmetry and find sharp bounds on the spectrum of the reduced density matrix of a single particle, as well as the exact set of spectra that mixed states can reach (for fermions these are the Pauli principle and Coleman's theorem).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA random Hall-Paige conjecture | Inventiones mathematicae | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00222-025-01328-x)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This implies that there is a bijection \\(\\phi \\colon X\\to Y\\) such that \\(x\\to x\\phi (x)\\) is injective. \\(\\phi \\) then corresponds to a near transversal in \\(M\\), as desired. ... The \\(p=1\\) case of Theorem 1.1 when applied with